  "verbalization": "Cefprozil, a broad-spectrum antibiotic, is linked to Streptococcal Infection through a chain of indications that involve bacterial infections. The evidence suggests that Cefprozil is used to treat Tonsillitis, which in turn is associated with Dirithromycin and Loracarbef, both of which are indicated for Streptococcal Infection. This implies that Cefprozil may have a role in treating infections caused by Streptococcus species through a pathway that involves the use of related antibiotics. The indirect connection is supported by the shared indication of bacterial infections, suggesting that Cefprozil may be repurposed for use in Streptococcal Infections through a mechanism that involves the use of other antibiotics in the same class. The evidence is consistent across multiple paths, highlighting a potential mechanism of action or therapeutic use that links Cefprozil to Streptococcal Infection.",
